      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-332635",
      ":question/problem": "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0, \u221e)$ and its Laplace transform\n$F(s) = L{f(t)}$\nexists for $s > a$. Then, $F(s)$ is differentiable at every point $s > a$, and we have\n$F^{\u2032}(s) =-L{t \\cdot f(t)}$.\n\nA proof of this statement is given below.\n\n> $L1$: $F(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\u221e}e^{-st}f(t)dt$ exists for $s > a$.\n> $L2$: $F^{\u2032}(s) = \\frac{d}{ds}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\u221e}e^{-st}f(t)dt$\n> $L3$: $F^{\u2032}(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\u221e}\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202s}(e^{-st}f(t))dt$\n> $L4$: $\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202s}(e^{-st}f(t)) =-te^{-st}f(t)$\n> $L5$: $F^{\u2032}(s) =-\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\u221e}te^{-st}f(t)dt$\n> $L6$: $F^{\u2032}(s) =-L{t \\cdot f(t)}$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nThe integral in line $L1$ converges since\n{{field-1}}.\n\nLine $L3$ follows from $L2$ and {{field-2}}.\n\nLine $L4$ follows from {{field-3}}."
    },
